Here’s a compact way to wire everything up in **one** initialization call:

```javascript
// Assuming you already have:
// - `initialStateObject`: your starting state
// - `reducersMap`: an object whose values are reducer functions
// - `loggerMiddleware`: a logging‑middleware function
// - `MyStore`: the underlying Redux/Redux‑like store instance  
// - `MyContext`: the React Context created for the store
// - `useStateSelectorHook` & `useDispatchActionHook`: your custom hooks

const rootStore = manageReactState(
  {
    /* ---------- Config Object ---------- */
    initialState: initialStateObject,
    reducers:       reducersMap,
    middlewares:    [loggerMiddleware],
    enhancers:      [applyMiddleware('myMiddleWare')]   // <-- enhancer
  },

  /* ---------- Store Instance ---------- */
  MyStore,

  /* ---------- Context Provider ----------- */
  MyContext,

  /* ---------- Hook Mapping ------------ */
  {
    selector: useStateSelectorHook,   // used inside components via `useSelector`
    dispatcher: useDispatchActionHook // used inside components via `useDispatch`
  }
);
```

> *What happens here?*  
> • The **configuration object** tells `manageReactState` what the initial state looks like, how each slice of state should change (`reducers`), what side‑effects/middleware to run on every action (`middlewares`) and any additional store enhancements (`enhancers`).  
> • The next two arguments supply the concrete store implementation and the React context that will expose that store throughout the component tree.  
> • Finally, the last argument maps your custom hooks so that components can read from and update the global state without directly touching the store API.  

With this single line executed at the top level of your app (e.g., right after creating the store and context) you’ll have a fully wired, performant, shared‑state solution ready for consumption across all React components.

